		\Omega = [0,1] 
	}
	as the set of the outcomes, and to identify the event "$X \in I$" with the interval $I$. We wi
\end{model}




\begin{ExerciseList}
	\Exercise
	\label{exercise:game_2}
    (\cite{Ross} Chapter 2 Exercise 2) In an experiment, die is rolled continually until a 6 appears, at which point the experiment stops. What is the sample space of this experiment? Let $E_n$ denote the event " The experiment is completed before n rolls". Write it as a subset of the sample space. Describe $\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^\infty E_i\right)^c$.
	\Answer We encode the outcome "The first roll gave 4, the second 2, the third 6, and then the experiment stopped" in the string $426$: a digit is the result of a roll, its position is the number of the roll, and the string stops after the first 6. We also have to allow for the die never showing a 6, which corresponds to the infinite strings of digits between 1 and 5, such as $121212\ldots$ Therefore 
	\bel{}{\Omega = \{6, 16, 26, 36, 46, 56, 116, \ldots\} \cup \{x_1 x_2 \ldots x_n \ldots \,, x_i \in \{1,2,3,4,5\}\} = E \cup G.} 
	The set $E$ is the event "The experiment stops after finitely many rolls" and $G$ is the event "The experiment never stops". As in Model~\ref{ex:infinite_coin}, the strings of $G$ are outcomes rather than events, since none of them can be decided with finitely many rolls; for any reasonable probability $G$ has probability $0$, but for other games one should take into account the event that the game never ends and make sure it has probability 0. 
	The events $E_n$ are $E_1 = \{6\}$, $E_2 = \{6, 16, 26, \ldots, 56\}$, $E_3 = E_2 \cup \{116, 126, \ldots, 556\}$, and in general 
	\bel{}{E_n = \{x_1 \ldots x_m 6 \,, x_j \in \{1, \ldots, 5\}, m \leq n - 1\},} 
	the strings of length at most $n$. By definition of "or", $E_1 \cup E_2 \cup E_3 \cup \ldots$ is the event "$E_n$ occurs for at least one $n$", that is, "The experiment stops after finitely many rolls", which is $E$. Its complement is $G$.

The event $$\bigcup_{i=1}^\infty E_n= E_0\cup E_1\cup E_2,....= "E_0\,\, or E_1 \,\,or ....E_n \,\,\,or ..."$$
is, by definition of "or", the event that $E_n$ happens for at least one $n$. That is, is the event "The game ends before $n$ rolls at least one $n$ "="The game ends after $n$ rolls for some $n$", which coincides with $E$.
The set event $E^c$ corresponds to $G$. 

\Exercise (\cite{Ross} Chapter 2 Exercise 3)
Two dice are thrown. Let $E$ be the event that the sum of the dice is odd, and $F$ the event that at least one of the two dice lands in $1$ and let $G$ be the event that the sum is 5. Describe the events $E\cap F $, $E\cup F$, $F\cap G$, $E\cap F^c=E\setminus F$ and $E \cap F\cap G$

\Answer
 
	\Answer Writing $ij$ for "The first die shows $i$ and the second $j$", 
	\bel{}{E = \{12, 14, 16, 21, 23, 25, 32, \ldots, 65\}, \qquad F = \{11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 21, 31, 41, 51, 61\}, \qquad G = \{14, 23, 32, 41\}.} 
	Then $E \cap F$ = "One die shows 1 and the other an even number" $= \{12, 14, 16, 21, 41, 61\}$; $E \cup F$ = "The sum is odd or a die shows 1" $= \{11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 21, 23, 25, 31, 32, 34, \ldots, 65\}$; $F \cap G$ = "One die shows 1 and the other 4" $= \{14, 41\}$; $E \cap F^c$ = "The sum is odd and no die shows 1" $= \{23, 25, 32, 34, 36, 43, 45, 52, 54, 56, 63, 65\}$; finally $G \subset E$, since a sum equal to 5 is odd, so that $E \cap F \cap G = F \cap G = \{14, 41\}$.


