\Exercise 
\label{e:game}(\cite{Ross} Chapter 2 Exercise 4)
Anton, Barbara  and Carlo take turns flipping a coin. The first one to get head wins. The sample space to this experiment can be defined as $\Omega=\{1,01,001,0001,...\}\cup\{00000...\}$.
\Question Interpret the sample space.
\Question In terms of the sample space, write the following events: A="Anton wins", B="Barbara wins" and $(A\cap B)^c$.

	\Answer 
	\Question $1$ = "The first toss gave heads", so that Anton wins; $01$ = "The first toss gave tails and the second heads", so that Barbara wins; $001$ is the event that Carlo wins at the third toss, and so on. The string $000\ldots$ is the event "Heads never appears", so that the game never ends. 
	\Question Anton tosses at the first, fourth, seventh, \ldots turn, so he wins when the $1$ is in position $3k+1$ for some $k \in \mathbb N$: 
	\bel{}{A = \{1, 0001, 0000001, \ldots\}.} 
	In the same way, Barbara wins when the $1$ is in position $3k+2$: 
	\bel{}{B = \{01, 00001, 00000001, \ldots\}.} 
	Finally $(A \cup B)^c = \{001, 000001, \ldots\} \cup \{000\ldots\}$ is the event "Carlo wins or the game never ends".
\end{ExerciseList}





	Many of the examples seen so far consist on a random number $X$ about which we are uncertain (for instance if we have not obaerved it): the result of a die, the number of costumers in the shop, hte number of verbs in Anna Karenina... Statements such as " The number X is smaller than 5" are perfectly legit event and we now provide (in an informal manner) the algebra of events that we want to consider when we come across such random quantities.  

\begin{model}[One random variable]
	\label{ex:one_rv}

	\label{ex:unif}
	By typing on R the command \texttt{runif}, the computer gives us a number $X$.  
<<>>=
runif(1) 
@
	The events we are interested in are of the form "$X < 1/2$" or "$X \in [1/4, 5)$", that is, "$X \in I$" for an interval $I\subset \mathbb R$, together with their "and", "or" and "not". 
	We can see the events as subsets of the sample space $\mathbb R$, where $ x \in \mathbb R$ stands for the event "The output of the command is $x$".

	In this case, we know that the command gives us back a number between $0$ and $1$, so that the sample space is $[0,1]$. The event $X \in I$, where $I \subset [0,1]$ is an interval, is thus \emph{identified with} $I$ through \ref{e:abstract_to_concrete}. 

	For instance, the event "The output $X$ of the command is smaller that $1/2$" is identified with $[0,1/2]$. 
	Suppose now that we observe $n$ random variables $X_1, \ldots, X_n$ such as the $n$ dice rolls or the percentage of people with degrees in $n$ countries. The events we are interested are of the form     
	\begin{equation}
		\label{e:n_rv}
		\text{"$X_1 \in A_1$ and $X_2 \in A_2$ and \ldots and $X_n \in A_n$"}, \qquad A_1, \ldots, A_n \subset \mathbb R. 
	\end{equation}
	where $A_1, \ldots A_n \subset \mathbb R$ are intervals. The events we want to consider if interested only in $X_1, \ldots X_n$ is the algebra generated by the events \eqref{e:n_rv}. We next give a basic example of $n$ random variables 

\begin{model}[$n$ random variables]
	\label{ex:n_rv}
<<>>=
sample(c(0, 1), 1)                           # a coin toss: a Bernoulli random variable
x <- sample(c(0, 1), 10, replace = TRUE)     # ten coin tosses
paste(x, collapse = "")                      # the outcome as a string of Omega_10
sum(x)                                       # the number of successes: the k of the E_k that occurred
y <- sample(1:6, 5, replace = TRUE)          # five rolls of a die, S = {1, ..., 6}
paste(y, collapse = "")
sample(c("formal", "dialectal"), 3, replace = TRUE)   # values that are not numbers
@
	The events can be seen as subsets of a sample space by considering
	\begin{equation}
		\label{e:n_rv_strings}
		\Omega = \{ s_1 s_2 \ldots s_n \,, s_i \in S \text{ for $i = 1, \ldots, n$} \} = S^n, 
	\end{equation}
	where $s_1\ldots s_n$ is the event "The first number $X_1$ is $s_1$ and ... and the $n$-th number is $s_n$". In this way, the event is
	which has $m^n$ elements if $S$ has $m$ elements. The event "$X_i \in A$" is the set of the strings whose $i$-th symbol is in $A$ and, when the values are numbers, "$X_1 + \ldots + X_n = k$" is the set of the strings whose digits sum to $k$, as in \eqref{e:ntoss_ksuccesses}. 
\end{model}


\begin{remark}[Bernoulli trials]
	\label{r:bernoulli_rv}
	A random variable with values in $\{0, 1\}$ is called a \emph{Bernoulli random variable}: it records whether something happened ($1$, a success) or not ($0$). 

	In R, the command \texttt{sample} produces the value of a random variable with values in a finite set $S$ (in this case \texttt{c(0,1)} )
<<>>=
sample(c(0, 1), 1)                           # a coin toss: a Bernoulli random variable
@
	The Bernoulli trial models introduced in Model~\ref{},~\ref{},~and~\ref{} are particular instaces of Model~\ref{ex:one_rv},~\ref{ex:n_rv} and ~\ref{ex:inf_rv}, respectively, when the variable $X_i$ are Bernoullian. 
	Indeed, in this case the sample spaces \eqref{e:rv_s}, \eqref{e:} and \eqref{e:} coincide with and we. We can simulate coin tosses using R: 
  
	In R, the command \texttt{sample} produces the value of a random variable with values in a finite set $S$, 
<<>>=
x <- sample(c(0, 1), 10, replace = TRUE)     # ten coin tosses
paste(x, collapse = "")                      # the outcome as a string of Omega_10
@
	The random number of Model~\ref{ex:unif} is \texttt{runif(1)}, and \texttt{runif(n)} gives $n$ of them. 
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[The standard definition of random variable]
	\label{r:standard_rv}
	Most textbooks define a random variable as a function  $X:\Omega \to \mathbb R$. $\Omega$ is the set of elementary events, and the fact that $X$ is a function of $\Omega$ means that once we make an observation $\omega \in \Omega$ we know the value of the random number $X(\omega)$.  
        As an example, the number of heads in $n$ coin tosses is a random variable in the space of $n$-coin tosses $\Omega_n$ of Model~\ref{ex:n_coin}, since we can compute it as 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{e:rv_bernoulli}
		X(x_1 \ldots x_n) = x_1 + \ldots + x_n. 
	\end{equation}
<<>>=
x <- sample(c(0, 1), 10, replace = TRUE)     # ten coin tosses
paste(x, collapse = "")                      # the outcome as a string of Omega_10
sum(x)                                       # the number of successes: the k of the E_k that occurred
@
	Similarly, the $i$-th coin toss in the space of infinite coin tosses is a random variable: 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{e:rv_bernoulli}
		X_i(x_1 \ldots x_n) = x_i. 
	\end{equation}
	In practice, what happens is the converse: you observe the random numbers and then you define events using those: $x_1...x_n \in \Omega_n $ is he elementary event $ (X_1 = x_1) \ldots ( X_n = x_n=)$. 
\end{remark}
